{
  "image_name": "5bcfeb23645f3b7e2a2bc10043e55bcc.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a proposed mechanism in liver cancer cells, including cancer stem cells, in which increased rRNA methylation drives ribosome production and tumor-promoting behavior. A liver tumor is shown on the left, and the main schematic on the right traces two parallel pathways inside the cancer cell.\n\nIn the upper pathway, higher ZCCHC4 is linked to increased m6A modification of 28S rRNA, which appears to raise formation of the large 60S ribosomal subunit. In the lower pathway, higher METTL5–TRMT112 is linked to increased m6A on 18S rRNA, promoting the small 40S subunit. These two changes converge on enhanced ribosome synthesis.\n\nThe extra ribosomes then support stronger protein translation in two ways: increased translation of 5′ TOP mRNAs and an increase in global mRNA translation. The diagram further connects this translational boost to altered fatty acid metabolism, which is placed downstream of the 5′ TOP branch and alongside the broader translation increase.\n\nOverall, the image presents a model where rRNA methylation enzymes enhance ribosome biogenesis and translational output, helping fuel liver cancer tumorigenesis, proliferation, and metastasis.",
